-- Theory questions for MTH 101. Safe to run twice.
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Functions$q$, $q$Define a function. Distinguish between a one-to-one function and an onto function, giving one example of each.$q$, 5, $q$A function f from a set A to a set B is a rule that assigns to every element x of A exactly one element f(x) of B. A is the domain and B the codomain. A function is one-to-one (injective) if different inputs always give different outputs, that is f(a) = f(b) implies a = b. Example: f(x) = 2x + 1 on the real numbers. A function is onto (surjective) if every element of the codomain is the image of at least one element of the domain. Example: f(x) = x^3 from the real numbers to the real numbers, since every real number has a real cube root.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition: every element of the domain has exactly one image","m":1},{"t":"One-to-one: different inputs give different outputs (f(a) = f(b) implies a = b)","m":1},{"t":"A correct example of a one-to-one function","m":1},{"t":"Onto: every element of the codomain is an image of some element","m":1},{"t":"A correct example of an onto function","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define a function. Distinguish between a one-to-one function and an onto function, giving one example of each.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Sets$q$, $q$State De Morgan's law for the complement of a union of two sets and prove it.$q$, 5, $q$De Morgan's law states that the complement of (A union B) equals (A complement) intersection (B complement). Proof. Let x be in the complement of (A union B). Then x is not in A union B, so x is not in A and x is not in B. Hence x is in A complement and in B complement, so x is in their intersection. This shows the left side is contained in the right side. Conversely, let x be in (A complement) intersection (B complement). Then x is not in A and not in B, so x is not in A union B, so x is in the complement of (A union B). The two sets contain each other, so they are equal.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Correct statement of the law","m":1},{"t":"Takes an element of the left side and shows it is in the right side","m":2},{"t":"Takes an element of the right side and shows it is in the left side","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State De Morgan's law for the complement of a union of two sets and prove it.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, $q$Solve x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0 by factorisation. Define the discriminant of a quadratic equation and use it to describe the nature of the roots here.$q$, 5, $q$Factorise: x^2 - 5x + 6 = (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3. For ax^2 + bx + c = 0 the discriminant is D = b^2 - 4ac. If D is greater than 0 the roots are real and distinct, if D = 0 the roots are real and equal, and if D is less than 0 the roots are not real. Here D = (-5)^2 - 4(1)(6) = 25 - 24 = 1, which is positive, so the roots are real and distinct. This agrees with the roots 2 and 3.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Correct factorisation (x - 2)(x - 3)","m":1},{"t":"Roots x = 2 and x = 3","m":1},{"t":"Discriminant defined as b^2 - 4ac","m":1},{"t":"Computes D = 1 for this equation","m":1},{"t":"States that D greater than 0 means real distinct roots","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Solve x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0 by factorisation. Define the discriminant of a quadratic equation and use it to describe the nature of the roots here.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Limits$q$, $q$Explain what is meant by the limit of a function as x approaches a number. Evaluate the limit of (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) as x approaches 2.$q$, 5, $q$The limit of f(x) as x approaches a is the value that f(x) gets closer and closer to as x gets closer to a, without having to equal a. For (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) the function is not defined at x = 2 because the denominator is zero. For x not equal to 2, factorise the numerator: x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2). Cancel (x - 2) to get x + 2. As x approaches 2, x + 2 approaches 4. So the limit is 4, even though the function itself is undefined at x = 2.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Idea of a limit: f(x) approaches a value as x approaches a, without needing f(a)","m":1},{"t":"Notes that direct substitution gives 0/0 or an undefined result","m":1},{"t":"Factorises x^2 - 4 as (x - 2)(x + 2)","m":1},{"t":"Cancels the common factor because x is not equal to 2","m":1},{"t":"Correct answer 4","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Explain what is meant by the limit of a function as x approaches a number. Evaluate the limit of (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) as x approaches 2.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Binomial theorem$q$, $q$State the binomial theorem for a positive integer power n and use it to expand (1 + x)^4.$q$, 5, $q$For a positive integer n, (a + b)^n = the sum from r = 0 to n of C(n, r) a^(n - r) b^r, where C(n, r) = n!/(r!(n - r)!). For (1 + x)^4 the coefficients are C(4,0) = 1, C(4,1) = 4, C(4,2) = 6, C(4,3) = 4 and C(4,4) = 1. So (1 + x)^4 = 1 + 4x + 6x^2 + 4x^3 + x^4.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Statement of the theorem with binomial coefficients C(n, r)","m":2},{"t":"Formula for C(n, r) = n!/(r!(n - r)!)","m":1},{"t":"Coefficients 1, 4, 6, 4, 1","m":1},{"t":"Final expansion 1 + 4x + 6x^2 + 4x^3 + x^4","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the binomial theorem for a positive integer power n and use it to expand (1 + x)^4.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Continuity$q$, $q$State the three conditions for a function f to be continuous at x = a. Give one example of a function that fails these conditions at some point.$q$, 5, $q$f is continuous at x = a if (1) f(a) is defined, (2) the limit of f(x) as x approaches a exists, and (3) the limit equals f(a). Example of failure: f(x) = 1/x is not defined at x = 0, so it is not continuous there. Another example is a step function that jumps at a point, where the left and right limits are different so the limit does not exist.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Condition 1: f(a) is defined","m":1},{"t":"Condition 2: the limit of f(x) as x approaches a exists","m":1},{"t":"Condition 3: the limit equals f(a)","m":1},{"t":"A correct example of a function that fails at a point","m":1},{"t":"Explains which condition fails in the example","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the three conditions for a function f to be continuous at x = a. Give one example of a function that fails these conditions at some point.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Mathematical induction$q$, $q$Use mathematical induction to prove that 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2 for every positive integer n.$q$, 10, $q$Let P(n) be the statement 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2. Base case: for n = 1 the left side is 1 and the right side is 1(2)/2 = 1, so P(1) is true. Inductive step: assume P(k) is true, that is 1 + 2 + ... + k = k(k + 1)/2. Add (k + 1) to both sides. The left side becomes 1 + 2 + ... + k + (k + 1). The right side becomes k(k + 1)/2 + (k + 1) = (k + 1)(k/2 + 1) = (k + 1)(k + 2)/2. This is the formula with n = k + 1, so P(k + 1) is true. By induction, P(n) holds for every positive integer n.$q$, $q$[{"t":"States the claim P(n) clearly","m":1},{"t":"Base case n = 1 checked","m":2},{"t":"States the inductive assumption P(k)","m":2},{"t":"Adds (k + 1) and simplifies to (k + 1)(k + 2)/2","m":3},{"t":"Concludes by the principle of mathematical induction","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Use mathematical induction to prove that 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2 for every positive integer n.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Functions$q$, $q$Find the domain and the range of f(x) = square root of (x - 3).$q$, 5, $q$The square root is defined for real numbers only when the quantity inside is not negative, so x - 3 must be greater than or equal to 0, which gives x greater than or equal to 3. The domain is [3, infinity). A square root is never negative and it equals 0 when x = 3 and grows without bound as x increases. So the range is [0, infinity).$q$, $q$[{"t":"Condition x - 3 greater than or equal to 0","m":1},{"t":"Domain [3, infinity)","m":1},{"t":"Notes the square root is never negative","m":1},{"t":"Range [0, infinity)","m":1},{"t":"Gives a check such as f(3) = 0","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Find the domain and the range of f(x) = square root of (x - 3).$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Sets$q$, $q$Define a subset and the power set of a set. List all the subsets of {a, b, c} and state how many subsets a set with n elements has.$q$, 5, $q$A set A is a subset of B if every element of A is also an element of B. The power set of A is the set of all subsets of A. For {a, b, c} the subsets are: the empty set, {a}, {b}, {c}, {a, b}, {a, c}, {b, c} and {a, b, c}. That is 8 subsets. In general a set with n elements has 2^n subsets, because each element is either in a subset or not, giving 2 choices per element.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of a subset","m":1},{"t":"Definition of the power set","m":1},{"t":"All 8 subsets listed","m":2},{"t":"General result 2^n","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define a subset and the power set of a set. List all the subsets of {a, b, c} and state how many subsets a set with n elements has.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Sets$q$, $q$In a class of 60 students, 35 offer Mathematics, 30 offer Physics and 10 offer both. How many students offer neither subject?$q$, 5, $q$Use n(M union P) = n(M) + n(P) - n(M and P). So n(M union P) = 35 + 30 - 10 = 55. These 55 students offer at least one of the two subjects. The number who offer neither is 60 - 55 = 5. A Venn diagram shows 25 offering Mathematics only, 20 offering Physics only, 10 offering both and 5 outside both circles.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Formula n(M union P) = n(M) + n(P) - n(M and P)","m":2},{"t":"n(M union P) = 55","m":1},{"t":"Neither = 60 - 55 = 5","m":1},{"t":"Correct Venn diagram values (25, 10, 20, 5)","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$In a class of 60 students, 35 offer Mathematics, 30 offer Physics and 10 offer both. How many students offer neither subject?$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Number systems$q$, $q$Define a rational number and an irrational number. Prove that the square root of 2 is irrational.$q$, 10, $q$A rational number can be written as p/q where p and q are integers and q is not 0. An irrational number cannot be written this way. Proof by contradiction: suppose the square root of 2 equals p/q in lowest terms, so p and q have no common factor. Squaring gives p^2 = 2q^2, so p^2 is even and therefore p is even. Write p = 2k. Then 4k^2 = 2q^2, so q^2 = 2k^2 and q is even too. Now p and q are both even, which contradicts the assumption that p/q is in lowest terms. So the square root of 2 is irrational.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of rational and irrational numbers","m":2},{"t":"Assumes sqrt(2) = p/q in lowest terms","m":2},{"t":"Shows p^2 = 2q^2 so p is even","m":2},{"t":"Shows q is also even","m":2},{"t":"States the contradiction and concludes","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define a rational number and an irrational number. Prove that the square root of 2 is irrational.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Inequalities$q$, $q$Solve the inequality x^2 - 3x - 4 < 0 and show the solution on a number line.$q$, 5, $q$Factorise: x^2 - 3x - 4 = (x - 4)(x + 1). The critical values are x = -1 and x = 4. The expression is negative when one factor is positive and the other negative. Test a point: at x = 0 the value is -4, which is negative, so the interval between the roots works. At x = -2 the value is 6 and at x = 5 the value is 6, both positive. So the solution is -1 < x < 4. On a number line this is an open interval from -1 to 4 with open circles at both ends.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Factorises as (x - 4)(x + 1)","m":1},{"t":"Critical values -1 and 4","m":1},{"t":"Tests points in each interval","m":1},{"t":"Solution -1 < x < 4","m":1},{"t":"Correct number line with open circles","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Solve the inequality x^2 - 3x - 4 < 0 and show the solution on a number line.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Partial fractions$q$, $q$Express (3x + 5)/((x + 1)(x + 2)) in partial fractions.$q$, 5, $q$Write (3x + 5)/((x + 1)(x + 2)) = A/(x + 1) + B/(x + 2). Multiply through by (x + 1)(x + 2) to get 3x + 5 = A(x + 2) + B(x + 1). Put x = -1: 2 = A(1), so A = 2. Put x = -2: -1 = B(-1), so B = 1. The result is 2/(x + 1) + 1/(x + 2). Check with x = 0: the left side is 5/2 and the right side is 2 + 1/2 = 5/2, which agrees.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Correct form A/(x + 1) + B/(x + 2)","m":1},{"t":"Equation 3x + 5 = A(x + 2) + B(x + 1)","m":1},{"t":"A = 2","m":1},{"t":"B = 1","m":1},{"t":"Checks the answer","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Express (3x + 5)/((x + 1)(x + 2)) in partial fractions.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Complex numbers$q$, $q$Define a complex number, its conjugate and its modulus. Compute (3 + 4i)(1 - 2i) and the modulus of 3 + 4i.$q$, 5, $q$A complex number is z = a + bi where a and b are real and i^2 = -1. Its conjugate is a - bi and its modulus is the square root of (a^2 + b^2). Multiply: (3 + 4i)(1 - 2i) = 3 - 6i + 4i - 8i^2 = 3 - 2i + 8 = 11 - 2i. The modulus of 3 + 4i is the square root of (9 + 16) = 5.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition z = a + bi with i^2 = -1","m":1},{"t":"Conjugate a - bi and modulus formula","m":1},{"t":"Expands the product correctly","m":1},{"t":"Product 11 - 2i","m":1},{"t":"Modulus 5","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define a complex number, its conjugate and its modulus. Compute (3 + 4i)(1 - 2i) and the modulus of 3 + 4i.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Complex numbers$q$, $q$Express z = 1 + i in polar form.$q$, 5, $q$The modulus is r = square root of (1^2 + 1^2) = square root of 2. The argument is theta = tan^-1(1/1) = 45 degrees, which is pi/4 radians, because the point (1, 1) is in the first quadrant. So z = sqrt(2)(cos(pi/4) + i sin(pi/4)).$q$, $q$[{"t":"Modulus sqrt(2)","m":2},{"t":"Argument pi/4 or 45 degrees with quadrant check","m":2},{"t":"Polar form sqrt(2)(cos(pi/4) + i sin(pi/4))","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Express z = 1 + i in polar form.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Trigonometry$q$, $q$Prove that sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) = 1 using a right-angled triangle and hence deduce that 1 + tan^2(x) = sec^2(x).$q$, 5, $q$In a right-angled triangle with opposite side a, adjacent side b and hypotenuse c, sin(x) = a/c and cos(x) = b/c. So sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) = (a^2 + b^2)/c^2. By Pythagoras a^2 + b^2 = c^2, so the sum is 1. Divide both sides of sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) = 1 by cos^2(x): tan^2(x) + 1 = 1/cos^2(x) = sec^2(x).$q$, $q$[{"t":"Defines sin and cos from the triangle","m":1},{"t":"Uses Pythagoras to get 1","m":2},{"t":"Divides by cos^2(x)","m":1},{"t":"States 1 + tan^2(x) = sec^2(x)","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Prove that sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) = 1 using a right-angled triangle and hence deduce that 1 + tan^2(x) = sec^2(x).$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Trigonometry$q$, $q$Solve 2sin^2(x) - sin(x) - 1 = 0 for values of x from 0 to 360 degrees.$q$, 5, $q$Let s = sin(x). Then 2s^2 - s - 1 = 0, which factorises as (2s + 1)(s - 1) = 0. So s = -1/2 or s = 1. For sin(x) = 1, x = 90 degrees. For sin(x) = -1/2, the reference angle is 30 degrees and sine is negative in the third and fourth quadrants, so x = 180 + 30 = 210 degrees and x = 360 - 30 = 330 degrees. The solutions are x = 90, 210 and 330 degrees.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Substitutes s = sin(x)","m":1},{"t":"Factorises (2s + 1)(s - 1)","m":1},{"t":"x = 90 degrees","m":1},{"t":"x = 210 degrees","m":1},{"t":"x = 330 degrees","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Solve 2sin^2(x) - sin(x) - 1 = 0 for values of x from 0 to 360 degrees.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Sequences and series$q$, $q$Define an arithmetic progression and state the formula for the sum of its first n terms. Find the sum of the first 20 terms of 3 + 7 + 11 + ...$q$, 5, $q$An arithmetic progression (AP) is a sequence in which each term differs from the previous one by a constant called the common difference d. The nth term is a + (n - 1)d and the sum of the first n terms is S_n = n/2 [2a + (n - 1)d]. Here a = 3 and d = 4. So S_20 = 20/2 [2(3) + 19(4)] = 10 [6 + 76] = 10 x 82 = 820.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of an AP with common difference","m":1},{"t":"Sum formula S_n = n/2 [2a + (n - 1)d]","m":1},{"t":"Identifies a = 3 and d = 4","m":1},{"t":"Substitutes correctly","m":1},{"t":"Answer 820","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define an arithmetic progression and state the formula for the sum of its first n terms. Find the sum of the first 20 terms of 3 + 7 + 11 + ...$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Sequences and series$q$, $q$State the condition for a geometric series to converge and find the sum to infinity of 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + ...$q$, 5, $q$A geometric progression has a common ratio r between consecutive terms. The infinite series converges only when the absolute value of r is less than 1. Its sum to infinity is S = a/(1 - r). Here a = 8 and r = 4/8 = 1/2, which is less than 1 so the series converges. S = 8/(1 - 1/2) = 8/(1/2) = 16.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Condition |r| less than 1","m":1},{"t":"Formula S = a/(1 - r)","m":1},{"t":"a = 8 and r = 1/2","m":1},{"t":"Answer 16","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the condition for a geometric series to converge and find the sum to infinity of 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + ...$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Functions$q$, $q$Given f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x^2, find f(g(x)), g(f(x)) and the inverse function of f.$q$, 5, $q$f(g(x)) = f(x^2) = 2x^2 + 3. g(f(x)) = g(2x + 3) = (2x + 3)^2 = 4x^2 + 12x + 9. These are different, so composition of functions is not commutative. To find the inverse of f, write y = 2x + 3, then solve for x: x = (y - 3)/2. So f^-1(x) = (x - 3)/2. Check: f(f^-1(x)) = 2((x - 3)/2) + 3 = x.$q$, $q$[{"t":"f(g(x)) = 2x^2 + 3","m":1},{"t":"g(f(x)) = 4x^2 + 12x + 9","m":1},{"t":"Notes they are not equal","m":1},{"t":"Inverse f^-1(x) = (x - 3)/2","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Given f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x^2, find f(g(x)), g(f(x)) and the inverse function of f.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Logarithms$q$, $q$State the laws of logarithms. Solve log base 2 of x plus log base 2 of (x - 2) = 3.$q$, 6, $q$Laws: log(ab) = log a + log b, log(a/b) = log a - log b and log(a^n) = n log a. Using the first law, log2(x) + log2(x - 2) = log2[x(x - 2)] = 3. So x(x - 2) = 2^3 = 8, which gives x^2 - 2x - 8 = 0. Factorise: (x - 4)(x + 2) = 0, so x = 4 or x = -2. Logarithms are only defined for positive numbers, and x = -2 would make log2(x) undefined, so it is rejected. The answer is x = 4.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Three laws of logarithms","m":2},{"t":"Combines the logs into log2[x(x - 2)]","m":1},{"t":"Forms x^2 - 2x - 8 = 0","m":1},{"t":"Solves to x = 4 or -2","m":1},{"t":"Rejects x = -2 with a reason","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$State the laws of logarithms. Solve log base 2 of x plus log base 2 of (x - 2) = 3.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 101$q$, $q$Elementary Mathematics I$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, $q$If alpha and beta are the roots of 2x^2 - 6x + 3 = 0, find alpha + beta, alpha x beta and alpha^2 + beta^2 without solving the equation.$q$, 5, $q$For ax^2 + bx + c = 0 the sum of the roots is -b/a and the product is c/a. Here a = 2, b = -6 and c = 3. So alpha + beta = 6/2 = 3 and alpha x beta = 3/2. Then alpha^2 + beta^2 = (alpha + beta)^2 - 2 alpha beta = 9 - 2(3/2) = 9 - 3 = 6.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Sum -b/a and product c/a","m":1},{"t":"Sum = 3","m":1},{"t":"Product = 3/2","m":1},{"t":"Uses (alpha + beta)^2 - 2 alpha beta","m":1},{"t":"alpha^2 + beta^2 = 6","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$If alpha and beta are the roots of 2x^2 - 6x + 3 = 0, find alpha + beta, alpha x beta and alpha^2 + beta^2 without solving the equation.$q$);
select course_code, count(*) as questions from public.theory_questions group by course_code order by course_code;
